CAT = "cbspdEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("A coworker signs off a sterilizer printout without reading it, to save time. What is the right response?",
 "Raise it now with them", ["Say nothing because the load looked fine", "Sign it again yourself later", "Mention it at next year's review"],
 "A signature on a printout states the cycle was checked. Signing without reading makes the record false, so it is raised promptly with the person or the lead."),
("A technician runs late and skips the rinse an HLD label requires, planning to do it next time. What does this break?",
 "The product label's rules", ["Only a local preference", "Nothing, if no one sees it", "The washer's warranty only"],
 "A disinfectant's label and the device IFU set required steps. Skipping one puts patients at risk and is a compliance failure, whatever the reason."),
("A new technician is unsure how to process an unfamiliar device. What is the professional step?",
 "Check its IFU or ask first", ["Process it like a similar device", "Leave it for the next shift", "Send it out without processing"],
 "Asking or checking the IFU before acting is the professional choice. Guessing by similarity can damage the device or leave it unsafe."),
("A technician finds a coworker's instruments cleaned but the coworker forgot a lumen brush step. The coworker is still on shift. What is best first?",
 "Tell the coworker so it is redone", ["File a formal complaint first", "Let the washer cycle catch it", "Redo it and tell no one"],
 "Most errors are fixed fastest by speaking to the person directly so the step is done. Repeated or unsafe patterns then go to the supervisor."),
("A coworker repeatedly bypasses a safety step after being reminded. What should happen now?",
 "Report it to the supervisor", ["Keep reminding them each day", "Do their work for them", "Wait for the annual review"],
 "When direct reminders have not worked and the practice is unsafe, the supervisor must know so it can be corrected."),
("A technician takes home a pack of sterile gauze for personal use. How is this classed?",
 "Theft of property", ["A perk of the job role", "Acceptable if it was spare", "Waste reduction"],
 "Taking facility supplies for personal use is theft, which the CBSPD outline lists as unprofessional behavior, however small the item."),
("Out of anger, a technician drops a tray on purpose and bends instruments. How is this classed?",
 "Willful damage", ["Normal wear and tear", "An ergonomic injury", "A packaging error"],
 "Deliberately damaging equipment is willful damage, a conduct issue separate from accidental breakage, which is reported and repaired."),
("A technician arrives wearing jewelry and street clothes that the dress code bans in the work area. What should a coworker do?",
 "Point out the policy kindly", ["Ignore it, it is not their job", "Wait for a lead to notice", "Copy them the next day"],
 "Professional peers help each other follow policy. A calm, respectful word is the right first step."),
("Two technicians argue loudly in front of OR staff about a missing instrument. What makes this unprofessional?",
 "It disrupts a care area", ["The instrument was not found", "The OR staff asked a question", "It happened on a night shift"],
 "Disruptive behavior is listed in the CBSPD ethics outline. Disagreements are resolved privately and calmly."),
("A technician accidentally contaminates a sterile tray. No one saw it. What is the ethical action?",
 "Send it to be reprocessed", ["Shelve it as sterile", "Wipe the wrap and shelve it", "Use it for a short case"],
 "Knowing a tray is contaminated, the honest action is to reprocess it, even when no one would find out."),
("A vendor offers a technician a personal gift card in return for using the vendor's product. What should the technician do?",
 "Decline the gift card", ["Accept it if it is small", "Accept and use the product", "Share it with the team"],
 "Personal gifts linked to product choices create a conflict of interest. Facilities set gift policies, and decisions must rest on patient safety."),
("A technician overhears a patient's diagnosis on a case cart call and is asked about it by a friend. What applies?",
 "Keep it confidential", ["Share it if the friend is family", "Share it if no name is used", "Share it once the patient leaves"],
 "Patient information is confidential. Sharing it outside care duties violates privacy rules and ethics."),
("A sterilizer is running fine, but its scheduled preventive maintenance is overdue. What should a technician do?",
 "Tell the supervisor", ["Keep using it until it fails", "Repair it themselves", "Log it at the next audit"],
 "Overdue maintenance is a compliance gap. The supervisor arranges service; technicians do not repair sterilizers themselves."),
("A technician notices a regulation-required log was left blank for three days. What is the right response?",
 "Report the gap as found", ["Fill in the blank days later", "Ask a coworker to fill it in", "Leave it, the auditor may not look"],
 "Backfilling or removing records makes them false. The gap is reported and documented as found so the cause can be fixed."),
("A technician is asked to process a device that is labeled single-use. The facility has no approved program. What should they do?",
 "Decline and tell the lead", ["Process it like a reusable device", "Reuse it once only", "Rinse it and return it"],
 "Reprocessing single-use devices makes the reprocessor a manufacturer under FDA rules. Without an approved program, the technician does not do it."),
("A manager asks a technician to release an implant load before its biological indicator result, with no emergency documented. What applies?",
 "Question the request", ["Release it as asked", "Release it if the CI changed", "Throw the implant away"],
 "CDC calls for quarantining implants until the BI is negative whenever possible. An early release needs documented justification under policy, so the request is questioned."),
]
